
AGIL DESCRIPTION -

Agıls  are generally built on an incline, in order to enable rain run-off; and, they face south. They are simple structures built exclusively of stone or mudbrick walls (c. one-meter high), with stone foundations. As in village houses, the wooden beams of the roof structure are supported by one or two vertical posts.  The agıl is rectangular, and open in the front leading to an unroofed courtyard where the sheep are milked and sleep at night. There is usually a large pile of sheep droppings which are periodically scraped off into a pile in one corner of the courtyard. The whole complex has one entrance; included in the complex are also a shepherd’s hut which is a free-standing building next to the agıl.  In shepherds hut, there is a fireplace directly across from the entrance. It consists of one room which measures c. 3x3 m. It is roofed. An agıl  accomodates on the average 400 animals (roofed); 800 (unroofed). .
	

